Same algorithm: split the text on whitespace, count each word in a hash map, take the most frequent word and add its count, its hash and the number of distinct words. The text is generated by the benchmark harness (the reference's generator and random sequence, byte for byte) and given to prepare, because the language cannot yet build a string from a byte array; prepare is untimed in both. The reference's HashMap<&str, u32> is lib/strmap.mito (chained, string keys up to 64 bytes). The leader is tracked while counting rather than found by a pass over the finished map (same word here, the maximum count is unique); this saves the reference's pass over 18,623 entries. Each run copies the 325 KB text into the request's text area first (a string field is read by copying), which the reference does not. Stores are checked and undo-logged.
